As per the regulations in Title 12, Chapter I, specifically 12 CFR 9, Subpart A, National Banks and Federal Savings Associations as Lenders, 9.8, Fiduciary Activities, a national bank must review and approve the fiduciary account before accepting it.

According to 12 CFR 9.8(a), a national bank must review and approve the fiduciary account before accepting it, and the bank must also obtain the consent of the fiduciary before accepting the account. 

12 CFR 9.8(a) states: "A national bank may accept a fiduciary account only after it has reviewed and approved the account and has obtained the consent of the fiduciary." 

Additionally, 12 CFR 9.8(b) states that the bank must also consider the following factors when reviewing and approving the fiduciary account:

"(1) The fiduciary's qualifications and experience;
(2) The fiduciary's ability to manage the account;
(3) The fiduciary's ability to provide adequate accounting and reporting;
(4) The fiduciary's ability to comply with applicable laws and regulations;
(5) The fiduciary's ability to maintain the confidentiality of the account information;
(6) The fiduciary's ability to avoid conflicts of interest;
(7) The fiduciary's ability to manage the account in accordance with the terms of the account agreement;
(8) The fiduciary's ability to provide adequate protection for the account assets;
(9) The fiduciary's ability to comply with the bank's policies and procedures for fiduciary activities;
(10) The fiduciary's ability to provide adequate accounting and reporting to the account beneficiaries."

Therefore, a national bank must review and approve the fiduciary account before accepting it, and the bank must also consider the factors listed in 12 CFR 9.8(b) when reviewing and approving the account.

