As of my cut-off knowledge in 2023, Title 12, Chapter I, specifically 12 CFR Part 21, 12 CFR Part 208, and 12 CFR Part 225, govern bank regulations. However, the specific requirements for BSA compliance procedures are outlined in 12 CFR Part 21, 12 CFR Part 208, and 12 CFR Part 225, as well as 12 CFR Part 323, which is the BSA/AML regulation.

According to 12 CFR Part 323, Subpart G, a bank must have a written BSA/AML compliance program that is approved by the bank's board of directors. The program must be designed to ensure and monitor compliance with the BSA and the bank's BSA/AML policies and procedures. (12 CFR 323.120(a))

The written program must include, at a minimum:

1. A description of the bank's BSA/AML policies and procedures (12 CFR 323.120(b)(1));
2. A description of the bank's risk-based approach to BSA/AML compliance (12 CFR 323.120(b)(2));
3. A description of the bank's customer due diligence procedures (12 CFR 323.120(b)(3));
4. A description of the bank's suspicious activity reporting procedures (12 CFR 323.120(b)(4));
5. A description of the bank's recordkeeping and reporting requirements (12 CFR 323.120(b)(5));
6. A description of the bank's internal controls and audit procedures (12 CFR 323.120(b)(6));
7. A description of the bank's training program for employees (12 CFR 323.120(b)(7));
8. A description of the bank's independent testing program (12 CFR 323.120(b)(8));
9. A description of the bank's BSA/AML compliance officer's responsibilities (12 CFR 323.120(b)(9));
10. A description of the bank's procedures for reporting BSA/AML compliance issues to the board of directors (12 CFR 323.120(b)(10)).

The board of directors must approve the written program and review it at least annually to ensure that it remains effective. (12 CFR 323.120(c))

Please note that these requirements may be subject to change, and it is essential to consult the most recent version of the regulations for the most up-to-date information.

