According to 12 CFR 34.27, lenders must provide notice to borrowers when collateral for a loan is located in a special flood hazard area. This is in accordance with the National Flood Insurance Act of 1968 and the Flood Disaster Protection Act of 1973.

12 CFR 34.27 states that:

"(a) Notice to borrowers. A lender shall provide a notice to a borrower, in writing, at the time of application for a loan, if the collateral for the loan is located in a special flood hazard area. The notice shall be in a form approved by the Director and shall include the following information:

(1) A statement that flood insurance is required for the loan;
(2) A statement that the borrower has the option to purchase flood insurance through the National Flood Insurance Program (NFIP) or through a private insurer;
(3) A statement that the lender will require the borrower to purchase flood insurance if the borrower does not purchase it;
(4) A statement that the borrower's failure to purchase flood insurance may result in the lender declaring the loan in default and accelerating the loan;
(5) A statement that the borrower may be eligible for a reduced annual premium rate if the borrower purchases flood insurance through the NFIP; and
(6) A statement that the borrower may be eligible for a reduced annual premium rate if the borrower purchases flood insurance through a private insurer that has a participating agreement with the NFIP."

This regulation is in place to ensure that borrowers are aware of the flood risk associated with their loan and to provide them with information about their options for flood insurance.

